===Etymology===
{{etymon|en|id=deed|:inh|enm:fact<ety:bor<la:factum<id:participle>>>|tree=1}}
{{inh+|en|enm|fact}}, from {{der|en|la|factum||an act, deed, feat, etc.}}; also [[Medieval Latin]] for “state, condition, circumstance”; neuter of {{m|la|factus||done or made}}, [[perfect passive participle]] of {{m|la|faciō||do, make}}, ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|*dʰeh₁-||to put, place, set}}. Old/Middle French later evolved it into [[faict]] and [[fait]]. <!-- Century 1911: The English words derived from "facere" are many.-->{{doublet|en|feat}}.

