===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# Something actual as opposed to invented.
#: {{co|en|establish the '''facts'''}}
#: {{co|en|hard '''facts'''}}
#: {{co|en|'''facts''' and figures}}
#: {{ux|en|In this story, the Gettysburg Address is a '''fact''', but the rest is fiction.}}
#: {{ux|en|The report is based on verified '''facts'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|Let’s stick to the '''facts''', not opinions.}}
#: {{ux|en|She presented the '''facts''' in a clear, logical way.}}
#* {{RQ:Travers Cuckoo in the Nest|chapter=2|passage=Mother{{...}}considered that the exclusiveness of Peter's circle was due not to its distinction, but to the '''fact''' that it was an inner Babylon of prodigality and whoredom, from which every Kensingtonian held aloof, except on the conventional tip-and-run excursions in pursuit of shopping, tea and theatres.}}
# Something which is real.
#: {{ux|en|Gravity is a '''fact''', not a theory.}}
# Something concrete used as a basis for further interpretation.
#: {{ux|en|Let's look at the '''facts''' of the case before deciding.}}
# An [[objective]] [[consensus]] on a [[fundamental]] [[reality]] that has been agreed upon by a substantial number of [[experts]].
#: {{ux|en|There is no doubting the '''fact''' that the Earth orbits the Sun.}}
# Information about a particular subject, especially actual conditions and/or circumstances.
#: {{ux|en|The '''facts''' about space travel.}}
#: {{ux|en|2=Addition '''facts''' include 2 + 2 = 4 and 3 + 4 = 7.}}
# {{lb|en|databases}} An individual [[value]] or [[measurement]] at the lowest level of [[granularity]] in a [[data warehouse]].
# {{lb|en|archaic}} [[action|Action]]; the realm of action.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1622|author=w:Francis Bacon|title=The History of the Reign of King Henry the Seventh|passage=After that Richard, the third of that name, king in '''fact''' only, but tyrant both in title and regiment{{...}}was{{...}}overthrown and slain at Bosworth Field; there succeeded in the kingdom{{...}}Henry the Seventh.|page=1}}
# {{lb|en|legal|obsolete|_|except in set phrases}} A wrongful or criminal deed.
#: {{ux|en|He had become an accessory after the '''fact'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|III|ix|passage=She was empassiond at that piteous act, / With zelous enuy of Greekes cruell '''fact''', / Against that nation [...].}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Measure|III|ii|76|2|His friends still wrought Repreeves for him: And indeed his '''fact''' till now in the government of Lord ''Angelo'', came not to an undoubtfull proofe.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1819|author=T. Howell|title=A Complete Collection of State Trials and Proceedings for High Treason and Other Crimes and Misdemeanors|passage=Gentlemen of the Jury, I think I need say but little on this matter: They all confess the '''fact''' of which they stand indicted. Some of them were old offenders, and all of them were proved to be at the taking of capt. Manwareing's sloop, and all took their shares: so that I think the '''fact''' is very fully and clearly proved upon them.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A {{l|en|feat}} or meritorious deed.
#* {{RQ:Marlowe Tamburlaine|part=1|scene=ii|page=45|passage=UUho when he ſhal embrace you in his arms<br>UUil tell how many thouſand men he ſlew.<br>And when you looke for amorous diſcourſe,<br>Will rattle foorth his '''facts''' of war and blood:<br>Too harſh a ſubiect for your daintie eares.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=w:Paradise Lost|author=w:John Milton|year=1667|passage=When he who most excels in '''fact''' of arms,}}

====Usage notes====
The collocation ''true fact'' is a famous example of a [[pleonasm]] that irritates some readers or listeners: All facts are true, and anything not true is not a fact. The collocation often arises innocently enough as a speaker or writer reaches for [[emphasis]]; but, in careful ([[deliberative]]) writing, the writer's own interests (''e.g.'', credibility, persuasion) are best served by deleting the adjective. Ironic usage may bend this rule for humorous effect (''e.g.'', ''[[trufax]]'').

